## Criticisms (crits)
[fa94c8f89b662939] (mini.criticism.v1)
The proposer misread the rule, not a rule/code divergence. The proposal names the kernel `open:creib.forge.conformance.oracle.recover_json_object`, but the documented kernel is `conformance.kernel.recovery`; the execution says that kernel could not read the input and rewritten text, so the expectation `moves` was never tested. The rule as written says 'The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text' and 'A candidate that parses as strict JSON, or as JSON with repeated keys resolved last-wins, is scoreable; one that does not (a float, for instance) is passed over'. The code implements this at `for pool in (fenced, top_level): for candidate in reversed(pool):` and `if type(value) is dict: return value, duplicates`. A proposer should build next a pair using the documented kernel `conformance.kernel.recovery` with a reply string as input, not the source function name.
[65fc2fe282f5e2f9] (mini.criticism.v1)
The proposal 678f984d3f62ecc1 expected a move but was unrunnable. The proposer misread the rule: the input string uses escaped newlines (literal backslash-n) rather than actual newlines, so the code fence regex does not match. The rule says "The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one" and the code line `_FENCE = re.compile('```(?:json|JSON)?\\s*(.*?)```', re.DOTALL)` requires actual whitespace after the fence marker, not a backslash-n sequence. The proposer should build next: provide the input and rewritten text with actual newline characters, not escaped `\n`, so the fence is recognised and the kernel can recover the object.

